videos.script:
A phone's screen is not a rectangle you own. {{screen}}

The clock and the battery sit across the top. A camera cuts into the glass. The corners are rounded, and a bar for gestures runs along the bottom. {{regions}} Then the user taps a text field, and a keyboard covers half of what is left. {{keyboard}}

Each of those regions is reported to the app as an inset: how far in from each edge the app should keep anything that matters. Draw under them on purpose, as a background. Keep text and buttons out of them. {{insets}} In this lesson you meet each inset, and how Android, iOS and React Native hand them to you. {{platforms}} Then a short program works out the safe part of an example screen, and asks one question about the planner. When the keyboard comes up, can the student still see the add session button? {{button}}
